  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "There are four more girls than boys in Ms. Raub’s class of \\(28\\) students. What is the ratio of the number of girls to the number of boys in her class?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( 3 : 4 \\)",
    "B": "\\( 4 : 3 \\)",
    "C": "\\( 3 : 2 \\)",
    "D": "\\( 7 : 4 \\)",
    "E": "\\( 2 : 1 \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Temporarily remove the four extra girls from the count. The remaining \\(28-4=24\\) students consist of equal numbers of girls and boys, so there are \\(24\\div2=12\\) boys. Restoring the four girls gives \\(12+4=16\\) girls.\n\nPut girls first in the requested ratio, then divide both terms by their greatest common factor, \\(4\\):\n\\[\n16:12=(16\\div4):(12\\div4)=4:3.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(4:3\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
